-Do not build; it takes too long, and poudriere checks the build. Reading
-metadata is fine. Fetch, extract, and the framework's metadata targets
-are allowed; configure, build, stage, install, and check-plist are
-not. Keep distfiles, work directories, and saved options out of the
-tree and out of the root-owned defaults, and take default options
-without prompting:
+Run make only through port-make.sh. It never builds a dependency:
+before make runs it checks the port's direct dependencies against the
+installed packages, and keeps STRICT_DEPENDS set so a missing one
+stops make rather than being built. It also keeps distfiles, work
+directories, and saved options under /var/tmp, out of the tree and the
+root-owned defaults, and takes default options without prompting:
 
     portmake() {
-        make -C "${tree}/${origin}" PORTSDIR="${tree}" BATCH=yes \
-            DISTDIR=/var/tmp/freebsd-ports-distfiles \
-            WRKDIRPREFIX=/var/tmp/freebsd-ports-work \
-            PORT_DBDIR=/var/tmp/freebsd-ports-db "${@}"
+        ~/lab/default.jj/oss/freebsd-ports/scripts/port-make.sh \
+            "${tree}" "${origin}" "${@}"
     }
     portmake makesum # writes distinfo from the fetched distfiles
     portmake extract # to read upstream's sources
     portmake cargo-crates # or gomod-vendor, as the framework requires
+    portmake stage check-plist # builds, stages, and checks the plist
+    portmake clean
 
-Never write a checksum by hand. Without a build the plist cannot be
-checked, so draft it from upstream's install rules and say in the patch
-notes that it is unverified.
+Never write a checksum by hand. Fix the port until `stage check-plist`
+passes; the build and plist are part of what the patch is judged on.
+
+Exit status 2 means dependencies are missing, and the output lists
+every one with a `pkg install` line. Do not work around it, and do not
+post the port. Answer with the full list instead, so the operator can
+install them in one step and run the skill again. When a list message
+asked for the port, that answer is the reply to it.
 
